#!/bin/sh
# A /bin/sh THAT ALWAYS INTERPOSES: it forks the command and stays resident, instead of
# exec-optimising itself away.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (#151). lib/ptyrun.py used to claim that `sh -c 'simple command'`
# exec-optimises, so the pty owner's direct child is the command and `pgrep -P` names it. That
# claim is not specifiable. Measured on macOS 26:
#
#   sh -c '…'              /bin/sh (bash 3.2)  /bin/dash (Apple dash-16)  /bin/ksh   /bin/zsh
#   sleep 5                replaces            replaces                   INTERPOSES replaces
#   sleep 5 >/dev/null     INTERPOSES          replaces                   INTERPOSES replaces
#   true && sleep 5        INTERPOSES          replaces                   INTERPOSES replaces
#
# It varies by shell, by BUILD of the same shell -- Ubuntu's dash 0.5.12 interposes where Apple's
# dash-16 does not, which is #151's whole report -- and by command shape.
#
# SO THE CONDITION IS INJECTED, NOT BORROWED FROM THE HOST. That is the rule #141 states in
# 14-test-harness.sh: a check that read the real environment would pass on a Mac, a CI runner and
# a plain Linux box "having measured nothing", and those are the machines this suite runs on. Do
# NOT reach for the host's own dash instead: on macOS /bin/dash EXISTS and OPTIMISES, so it proves
# the opposite of what a reader would expect. /bin/ksh there does interpose, and is recorded as a
# real-shell control beside the assertions that use this file.
#
# STDIN IS SAVED AND RESTORED EXPLICITLY, and that is the whole difficulty of these five lines.
# POSIX assigns /dev/null to an asynchronous list's stdin BEFORE any explicit redirection, so a
# bare `cmd &` hands the command NO TERMINAL -- measured, `test -t 0` false. That is not what dash
# does when it forks, and it would put every tty-dependent assertion behind this fixture against a
# launcher taking its no-terminal refusal: green for the wrong reason, or red for a reason that is
# this file's fault rather than the product's.
#
# AND FD 3 IS CLOSED IN THE CHILD. Left open it leaks into the command -- measured -- and 3 and
# 4 are run-tests.sh's own stdout and stderr (`exec 3>&1 4>&2`), with 9 held by cs193v-ui.sh's
# run_timeout. 10-static.sh asserts both of these properties are still here, for the same
# reason it asserts lib/sudo-fake has no exec branch: a "simplification" that deleted either one
# would leave the assertions that use this file passing while measuring nothing.
[ "$1" = "-c" ] || { echo "sh-fake: only -c is supported (got: ${1:-nothing})" >&2; exit 2; }
exec 3<&0
/bin/sh -c "$2" <&3 3<&- & kid=$!
wait "$kid"; exit $?
